  156: 
  157: With phase scoping, four missing first-decline cells can be appended to those accepting sessions. The remaining six require rearranging other sessions�?T first declines or adding processes. The repeat cell can simply be appended to the linear-width session.
  158: 
  159: Additional commands still increase run time and exercise the fixed-delay transcript machinery. Avoiding new processes does not make that cost zero.
  160: 
  161: For question 4, **I prefer an unconditional return in the PUT default**. The separate flag is unnecessary here, although it is not a defect in the current execution.
  162: 
  163: The concrete structure is:
  164: 
  165: ```c
  166: if (put) {
  167:         switch (color_mode) {
  168:         /* Existing accepting cases and their packing stay here. */
  169: 
  170:         default:
  171:                 if (!(d->warned & SGIRE_W_BADCOLOR)) {
  172:                         d->warned |= SGIRE_W_BADCOLOR;
  173:                         /* Existing fatal() call, unchanged. */
  174:                 }
  175:                 return;
  176:         }
  177: 
  178:         cpu->memory_rw(cpu, cpu->mem, tileptr + ofs,
  179:             buf, bufdepth, MEM_WRITE, NO_EXCEPTIONS | PHYSICAL);
  180: } else {
  181:         /* Existing GET branch. */
  182: }
  183: ```
  184: 
  185: The `return` must be **outside the warning-latch conditional**.
  186: 
  187: This has been checked against the actual enclosing function:
  188: 
  189: - The function returns `void`.
  190: - The accepting cases break to the one PUT store.
  191: - The default returns without calling that store.
  192: - The GET branch is an alternative branch, not subsequent work.
  193: - Nothing executable follows the PUT/GET branches.
  194: - Returning from this helper returns control to the caller�?Ts drawing loop; it does not terminate that loop.
  195: 
  196: The width-handler concern about skipping `memory_writemax64()` and `return 1` does not transfer to this helper. Those statements occur in `DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_re)`. [Helper�?Ts ending](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c:364), [Different handler�?Ts tail](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c:833)
  197: 
  198: The alternatives cost:
  199: 
  200: | Form | Benefit | Cost or limitation |
  201: |---|---|---|
  202: | Current `ok` flag | Correct no-store behavior today | Carries a decision to a later, separately editable guard without needing to do so. |
  203: | Unconditional default `return` | Makes rejection terminal and removes the flag | Small local refactor and comment updates. Future edits can still condition or remove the return; it is not mutation-proof. |
  204: | Initialize `buf`, retain rejection | Prevents uninitialized bytes from being copied if a store occurs | Changes additional width/read behavior; does not prevent an unauthorized store. |
  205: | Store inside each accepting case | Default has no route through a shared store | Four duplicated callback sites and argument lists; accepted-width initialization defects remain. |
  206: 
  207: No new behavioral row can distinguish a correctly placed early return from the correct current flag implementation: they are observationally equivalent here. Existing acceptance, decline, continuation and latch checks should remain applicable. I have not compiled or executed this refactor.
  208: 
  209: For question 5, **initializing the local buffer and zero-filling the destination are different decisions**.
  210: 
  211: With rejection intact, initializing `buf` does not write anything to the destination. The comment�?Ts objection to publishing an unsolicited colour therefore does not prohibit a local initializer.
  212: 
  213: However, initialization does **not** make a spurious store harmless. It converts a possible disclosure into a deterministic unauthorized overwrite. �?oUnsupported PUT stores nothing�?? remains a separate invariant.
  214: 
  215: There is a specific transferred warrant available here, stronger than �?oit cannot hurt�??: **accepted PUT paths already copy uninitialized bytes in the present worktree**.
  216: 
  217: | Accepted packing arm | Bytes initialized | Transfer depths with an uninitialized suffix |
  218: |---|---:|---|
  219: | CI | 1 | 2, 4, 8 |
  220: | RGB | 4 | 8 |
  221: | RGBA | 4 | 8 |
  222: | ABGR | 4 | 8 |
  223: 
  224: All these combinations can pass the colour switch and reach the store. No depth/type compatibility check prevents them. [Packing and store](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c:321)
  225: 
  226: The same file�?Ts #509 comment records the same buffer-to-guest failure mechanism and explicitly leaves other widths outstanding. That supports a **transferred warrant for initialization safety**, not a measured delta established by this review. [#509 warrant and remaining widths](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c:1539)
  227: 
  228: I would accept `uint8_t buf[8] = {0};` as a candidate companion repair on that stated warrant. I would not describe it as merely belt-and-braces for #519:
  229: 
  230: - It changes six accepted PUT format/depth combinations.
  231: - Placed at the existing shared declaration, it also changes GET behavior when the read supplies fewer bytes than packing consumes.
  232: - It establishes deterministic bytes, not hardware-correct semantics for every unsupported format/depth combination.
  233: - Its generated-code and performance cost have not been measured here.
  234: 
  235: The permitted files do not establish that zero padding is the intended hardware behavior for all those combinations. They do establish why copying uninitialized storage is unacceptable.
  236: 
  237: For question 6, **the distinction between `sgirandomcolour` and `sgirebufuninit` survives**.
  238: 
  239: They protect different invariants:
  240: 
  241: - `sgirandomcolour`: an unsupported PUT performs **no store**.
  242: - `sgirebufuninit`: every byte consumed by an actual transfer or colour reconstruction is **initialized**.
  243: 
  244: A mutant can violate both simultaneously. That does not make the invariants interchangeable.
  245: 
  246: An early return fixes the unsupported PUT path but leaves the accepted-width cases above. Initialization prevents disclosure through those bytes but does not make an unsupported store correct.
  247: 
  248: GET adds another independent initialization problem: RGB reconstruction consumes three bytes; RGBA, ABGR and the default reconstruction consume four. Depth-one and depth-two reads can supply fewer bytes than those expressions consume. The callback�?Ts return value is also ignored, although the permitted files do not establish its failure/partial-read guarantees.
  249: 
  250: Neither supplied probe exercises this helper�?Ts GET packing path. CPU loads of destination RAM and reads of RE register arrays are not calls to the pixel GET branch.
  251: 
  252: For question 7, the existing rows compare with the disclosed mutants as follows:
  253: 
  254: | Mutant | Existing row whose destination channel can differ | Reason |
  255: |---|---|---|
  256: | M30_tilenrdecline | `xy dec c1`, `c3`, `c4` | Their final tiled index is nonzero. |
  257: | M31_tilenrguard | `xy dec c1`, `c3`, `c4` | Same relevant state distinction. |
  258: | M32_tilenrxdecline | `xy dec c1`, `c4` | Their horizontal tile index is nonzero. |
  259: | M33_tiledorigin | `xy dec c2`, `c4` | They are tiled and their **masked** `y` is nonzero. |
  260: | M37_fgvalue, as described in the probe | The ten `nib �?�` decline rows | They use `FG_ALT`, rather than the foreground constant accepted by that mutant�?Ts condition. |
  261: | M28r | Not exactly assignable from these files | The probe describes M28 as a nibble restriction and adds ten corresponding decline rows, but supplies no exact M28r edit. |
  262: 
  263: In particular:
  264: 
  265: - C3 does **not** expose M33: incoming `y == 128` becomes zero before the colour switch.
  266: - `ycrcb x4y1` does **not** expose M30�?"M33 as written in your table: it is linear, and both relevant tile indices are zero there.
  267: - The coordinate **accept** rows do not kill default-only decline mutations. They establish that the chosen destination is reachable.
  268: - `xy ann` does not kill these store-only mutants when they leave the warning behavior intact.
  269: - `nib accept alt` establishes alternate-colour acceptance; it does not kill the stated M37 default-only mutation. The alternate-colour **decline** rows do that.
  270: 
  271: These are static distinctions, not newly measured kills.
  272: 
  273: Three oracle issues remain material.
  274: 
  275: **First, every existing depth-eight decline assertion observes only four bytes.** `cell_op()` paints one word and reads it with `lw`; `printed_seq()` normalizes to 32 bits. The `d8` names do not change that. [Cell operation and parser](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress/sgi_re_colourdefault_probe.py:434)
  276: 
  277: I would strengthen the existing `tA`, `tB`, `tC` and `lin ycrcb d8` assertions to paint and compare all eight bytes, using distinct nonzero contents across the two words. This need not add logical rows or sessions.
  278: 
  279: The named distinguishing control for each is `TAIL_WRITE(mode)`: an incorrect declined operation changes the upper-addressed four bytes while preserving the first four. The existing channel cannot vary under that control; the strengthened channel can.
  280: 
  281: **Second, unchanged paint is not proof of no store.** Uninitialized bytes can happen to equal the paint. Therefore, the proposed eleven rows and existing disclosure rows have a nonvacuous destination channel, but a source-only review cannot promise that each will kill its mutant on a particular build.
  282: 
  283: A test harness observing **zero write callbacks** would establish the refusal effect without copying or depending on uninitialized bytes. Such a harness is not present in these files. It is a separate testing capability, not something I can claim the existing sessions already provide.
  284: 
  285: **Third, the anchor and latch claims have narrower scopes than some comments suggest.**
  286: 
  287: `xy anchor t0` watches tile zero�?Ts word at offset zero. It does not watch every offset in tile zero. A translation error preserving a nonzero offset can leave that anchor unchanged; the per-cell accept rows provide the relevant detection for those ordinary misrouting cases.
  288: 
  289: Similarly, `allbits` establishes **other latches first, colour second**. The width probe�?Ts two-order TEX/unmapped example does not establish colour-first independence.
  290: 
  291: I would add one aggregate `colour/others` assertion to an existing colour-first session: after the colour announcement, drive the twelve other sites and require each expected announcement. Its named control family is `SET_OTHER(j)`: the colour warning update also sets another site�?Ts bit. The observable is the missing announcement from site `j`. The current other-first sequence can remain green under that error because the other bit was already set.
  292: 
  293: That adds one assertion and needs no new process. It complements, rather than repeats, the existing shared-bit control. [Existing colour priming](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress/sgi_re_colourdefault_probe.py:326), [Width probe�?Ts ordering precedent](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress/sgi_re_width_probe.py:570)
  294: 
  295: Finally, extending coordinates across depths requires recomputing and validating the selected entry and destination. Reusing C1�?Ts fixed vertex at another depth can select another tile; changing only the mode can consequently turn a proposed decline test into an earlier invalid-pointer return. Each added address/depth setup needs an accepting control that proves that same mapping reaches its intended destination.
  296: 
  297: The concrete review recommendation is therefore an unconditional default-arm return, an explicitly warranted treatment of buffer initialization, and a contract that names its actual partitions. The **eleven missing mode/depth/latch cells, one reverse-latch assertion, and strengthened four depth-eight assertions** are a bounded, reviewable improvement. They must not be represented as full closure over arbitrary predicates on all function inputs.

